/*
Original work, written for tests/fixtures/nonlinear-timeline (temporal-
consistency mechanism material, 2026-08-03). Mixed structure: a
FLASHBACK bracket (returning via CONTINUOUS) plus a separate forward
time-jump using a LATER heading modifier, per the markers
extractTemporalConstraints() actually parses. Temporally clean as
written -- 0 expected contradictions.
*/

INT. REGIONAL PLAYHOUSE - BACKSTAGE - NIGHT

Racks of costumes, a mirror ringed with bare bulbs. DELPHINE ASANTE, twenties, stares at her own reflection in a half-buttoned understudy's costume, hands not quite steady.

STAGE MANAGER (O.S.)
Five minutes, Delphine. You're on in five.

DELPHINE
(to the mirror)
Five minutes to become someone who's had four years of rehearsal I never got.

INT. REGIONAL PLAYHOUSE - WINGS - NIGHT

Delphine peers through the curtain gap at the packed house, the orchestra tuning below.

DELPHINE
(muttering)
Just don't think about the last time. Just don't.

INT. REGIONAL PLAYHOUSE - AUDITORIUM - NIGHT - FLASHBACK

Three years younger, YOUNG DELPHINE stands center stage under a single spotlight, mid-audition, voice cracking badly on a high note. A DIRECTOR watches from the dark house, expression unreadable.

DIRECTOR (O.S.)
Thank you. We'll be in touch.

Young Delphine's face falls, just for a second, before she catches it.

INT. REGIONAL PLAYHOUSE - LOBBY - CONTINUOUS

Young Delphine pushes through the lobby doors into the cold, phone already out, dialing.

YOUNG DELPHINE
(into phone, forcing brightness)
It went fine. I mean, it went. I'll know Thursday.

She hangs up before whoever answered can hear her voice actually break.

INT. REGIONAL PLAYHOUSE - WINGS - NIGHT - CONTINUOUS

Present day again: Delphine blinks hard, the memory releasing her. The stage manager appears at her shoulder, headset crooked.

STAGE MANAGER
Hey. Wherever you just went, come back. You're on.

DELPHINE
(steadying)
I'm back. I promise I'm back.

INT. REGIONAL PLAYHOUSE - WINGS - NIGHT

The stage manager squeezes her arm once, all business again.

STAGE MANAGER
The lead's understudy has never gone on cold in this house. You're about to be the exception.

DELPHINE
Exceptions get remembered. That's what I'm counting on.

INT. REGIONAL PLAYHOUSE - AUDITORIUM - NIGHT

The house lights dim. A ripple of anticipation moves through the crowd as the curtain begins to rise.

STAGE MANAGER (V.O.)
(over headset, to the booth)
Standby, house to half. She's going on.

INT. REGIONAL PLAYHOUSE - AUDITORIUM - NIGHT - FOUR YEARS LATER

The same house, renovated seats, a different marquee glow bleeding through the lobby doors. DELPHINE, now visibly assured, takes a bow to a full standing ovation, flowers landing at her feet.

VOICE FROM CROWD (O.S.)
(shouted, joyful)
Encore!

Delphine laughs, genuinely startled by the volume of it.

INT. REGIONAL PLAYHOUSE - BACKSTAGE - NIGHT

Delphine collapses into a chair in front of the same mirror, bulbs still bare, breathing hard, radiant.

DELPHINE
(to her reflection)
Four years. You'd think it would stop feeling like the first time.

A knock at the door. The CURRENT STAGE MANAGER pokes her head in, young, nervous, achingly familiar.

CURRENT STAGE MANAGER
Ms. Asante? Your understudy asked if she could watch you get ready. If that's allowed.

INT. REGIONAL PLAYHOUSE - BACKSTAGE - NIGHT

Delphine looks at the young understudy hovering in the doorway, recognizing something in the girl's face she hasn't seen in years -- not the costume, the fear underneath it.

DELPHINE
Tell her to come sit right here. I've got about five minutes' worth of advice I wish someone had given me.

The understudy steps in slowly, and Delphine pats the chair beside her own mirror, already making room.
